To handle simultaneous access to resources according to the notes at the end of the file, you can use a mutex (mutual exclusion object). A mutex is a synchronization mechanism that allows only one thread or process to access a shared resource at a time.

In this context, the specification file "broken_specs.txt" mentions using a mutex to ensure exclusive access to the shared log file. This means that when multiple threads try to write to the log file simultaneously, only one of them can do so at a time, and the others will have to wait until the first thread finishes writing before they can start.

The term "mutex" is derived from the Greek word "mutare," which means "to change." In this context, it refers to the ability to change or modify the state of a resource, such as the log file.
